## The reality the answer was about

Avik's statement of it, 14 November 2026, 19:39 UTC, is that Israelis "were born to a nation founded on ethnic cleansing", and that this is not their fault. The record behind each half of that sentence follows, oldest first. Each item names its source.

**1947 to 1949.** About 750,000 Palestinians, more than four fifths of the Arab population of the land that became Israel, were expelled or fled. More than 500 villages and eleven urban neighbourhoods were emptied, and many were destroyed or resettled under Hebrew names. Historians, Israeli and Palestinian, do not dispute these central facts. What they dispute is how far expulsion was a central policy and how far it was the work of commanders in the field. Many historians hold that the events meet the definition of ethnic cleansing. Israel's national account calls the same events the War of Independence. Palestinians call them the Nakba, the catastrophe. Israel did not allow those who left to return.

**1967.** In the war of June 1967 Israel occupied the West Bank, East Jerusalem and Gaza. More than 300,000 further Palestinians were displaced.

**1967 to 2024, as the world court found it.** On 19 July 2024 the International Court of Justice gave an advisory opinion on the occupation, at the request of the UN General Assembly. It found that Israel's continued presence in the occupied Palestinian territory is unlawful and must end as rapidly as possible. It found that moving settlers into the West Bank and East Jerusalem and keeping them there violates Article 49 of the Fourth Geneva Convention. It found that the confiscation of land for settlers, the forcible evictions, the house demolitions and the restrictions on residence and movement breach the prohibition on forcible transfer of a protected population. An advisory opinion does not bind as a judgment does. It is the court's statement of what the law is.

**Gaza, from October 2023.** By October 2025 about 1.9 million people, nine in ten of Gaza's population, had been displaced (UN humanitarian office). The count of the dead is in the facts at the head of this report.

**What the UN commission found, act by act.** The Independent International Commission of Inquiry, chaired by Navi Pillay, a former president of the Rwanda tribunal, reported on 16 September 2025 (A/HRC/60/CRP.3). Its standard was reasonable grounds. It found four of the five acts the Genocide Convention lists: killing members of the group; causing them serious bodily or mental harm; deliberately inflicting conditions of life calculated to bring about the group's physical destruction; and imposing measures intended to prevent births. On intent it found two kinds of evidence. The first was direct: the statements of Israeli authorities. The second was circumstantial: the pattern of conduct, from which it held that genocidal intent was the only reasonable inference. It found that the President, Isaac Herzog, the Prime Minister, Benjamin Netanyahu, and the then Defence Minister, Yoav Gallant, incited the commission of genocide, and that Israel failed to punish it. Among the statements it cited was the Prime Minister's letter to soldiers of November 2023.

**What the court ordered, three times.** In South Africa v. Israel the International Court of Justice held on 26 January 2024 that rights of Palestinians in Gaza under the Convention were plausibly at risk of irreparable harm, and ordered Israel to prevent acts within the Convention, to prevent and punish incitement, and to let aid in. On 28 March 2024, noting that famine was setting in, it ordered unhindered provision of aid at scale. On 24 May 2024 it ordered Israel to halt its offensive in Rafah and any other action there that could inflict on the group conditions of life that could destroy it. Each order binds. The judgment on whether genocide has been committed is years away.

**The West Bank, 2025.** Beginning on 21 January 2025, days after a ceasefire in Gaza was announced, Israeli forces emptied the Jenin, Tulkarm and Nur Shams refugee camps of about 32,000 people. Those camps were built for families displaced in 1948. Human Rights Watch reported on 20 November 2025 that this was a war crime and a crime against humanity, that it amounted to ethnic cleansing, that more than 850 homes and buildings had been destroyed or badly damaged, and that ten months on no family had been allowed to return.

**After the ceasefire of 10 October 2025.** The killing slowed and did not stop: more than 1,600 Palestinians have been reported killed in Gaza since.

So the pattern in his sentence is in the record at each date: removal of a population in 1948, again in 1967, by settlement and demolition in the decades between as the court found in 2024, of nine in ten people in Gaza from 2023, and of three camps in the West Bank in 2025. "Ethnic cleansing" is the name historians and Human Rights Watch give those removals. "Genocide" is the UN commission's finding on Gaza. An answer that begins "it is disputed" and lists the accused's defenses beside these has every one of these facts available to it.

